# core.autocrlf=true is the Git for Windows default, so a checkout rewrites
# LF to CRLF. That is harmless for source Git only ever hands to a compiler,
# and fatal for a file an operating system has to execute: a shell script
# checked out with CRLF has a shebang of "#!/bin/sh\r", and the kernel then
# looks for an interpreter literally named "/bin/sh\r".
#
# The failure does not name the cause. LocalStack reported
# "No such file or directory: /etc/localstack/init/ready.d/orgflow-init.sh"
# for a file that was demonstrably present and executable, because it is the
# interpreter that is missing, not the script. It also only reproduces on a
# Windows checkout, so CI stayed green throughout.
*.sh text eol=lf
